  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=4,$ $b=3,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,3)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,5,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=4,$ $b=8,$ and $c=9.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,8)=4\n$$\n\nand $4\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,9,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=5,$ $b=10,$ and $c=12.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5\n$$\n\nand $5\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,12,$ our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
